# Connecting via SSH

Thanks for setting up a VPS with Fyra Stack! If you haven't set up a VPS yet, see the section below on generating an SSH key. To connect to your server for the first time, you'll need a few things. First, you'll need an SSH client. Second, you'll need the public IP address of your VPS. Finally, you'll need the SSH key you set up for your VPS. If you haven't

## Generating an SSH Key

We recommend the ed-25519 algorithm for generating SSH keypairs as it is more secure and efficient than RSA. If you already have an SSH keypair, you can use that instead of generating a new one.

### On Linux or macOS

Open your terminal and run the following command to generate a new SSH key pair:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 
```

To get the public key, run the following command:

```bash
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

If you saved the key in a different location, look there and use `cat` on the file ending in `.pub`.

Copy the line beginning in `ssh-ed25519` and ending with your username. This is your public key, which you'll need to add to your VPS.

### On Windows
Modern Windows clients have the `ssh-keygen` command available in PowerShell. Open PowerShell and run the following command to generate a new SSH key pair:

```powershell
ssh-keygen -t ed25519
```

To get the public key, run the following command:

```powershell
Get-Content $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub
```

Copy the line beginning in `ssh-ed25519` and ending with your username. This is your public key, which you'll need to add to your VPS.

### Adding your public key to your VPS

On the Fyra Stack Dashboard, click on the settings icon and go to SSH Keys. From here, paste in your public key and give it a name. Do this *before* creating your VPS as it is added during the initialization stage.

## Setting up a client

### On Linux or macOS

Open your terminal and use the `ssh` command followed by root and the IP address of your VPS:

```bash
ssh root@your_vps_ip -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

### On Windows

You can use a terminal emulator like PowerShell or Command Prompt, or download an SSH client like PuTTY.

#### Using PowerShell

Open PowerShell and use the `ssh` command followed by root and the IP address of your VPS:

```powershell
ssh root@your_vps_ip -i $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519
```

#### Using PuTTY

1. Download and install PuTTY from [the official website](https://www.putty.org/).
2. Open PuTTY and enter the IP address of your VPS in the "Host Name (or IP address)" field.
3. In the "Connection" category, expand "SSH" and click on "Auth". Click "Browse" and select your private key file (the one without the `.pub` extension).
4. Click "Open" to start the SSH session.

## Optional - Making login easier

You can make logging in easier by adding your key to an SSH agent, configuring an SSH host shortcut, or both.

### Add your key to an SSH agent

An SSH agent keeps your private key unlocked for your current login session, so you do not need to type your key passphrase every time you connect.

#### On Linux or macOS

Start the agent and add your private key:

```bash
eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

After this, you can connect without passing `-i` each time:

```bash
ssh stackuser@your_vps_ip
```

On macOS, you can also store the key passphrase in your keychain:

```bash
ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

#### On Windows

Windows includes an OpenSSH Authentication Agent service. Open PowerShell as Administrator and run:

```powershell
Set-Service ssh-agent -StartupType Automatic
Start-Service ssh-agent
```

Then add your private key:

```powershell
ssh-add $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519
```

After this, you can connect without passing `-i` each time:

```powershell
ssh stackuser@your_vps_ip
```

### Create an SSH config shortcut

You can also create a shortcut in your SSH config file so you only need to type a short command like `ssh fyra-vps`.

#### On Linux or macOS

Create or edit `~/.ssh/config` and add:

```sshconfig
Host fyra-vps
  HostName your_vps_ip
  User stackuser
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
  AddKeysToAgent yes
```

Make sure the config file has the right permissions:

```bash
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/config
```

Then connect with:

```bash
ssh fyra-vps
```

#### On Windows

Create or edit `$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\config` and add:

```sshconfig
Host fyra-vps
  HostName your_vps_ip
  User stackuser
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
  AddKeysToAgent yes
```

Then connect with:

```powershell
ssh fyra-vps
```

Replace `fyra-vps` with any shortcut name you prefer, and replace `your_vps_ip` with your VPS public IP address.

